@page "/HowItWorks/shares-and-funds"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/shares-and-funds">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Where share and fund trades come from</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Interactive Brokers:</strong> orders with asset category "STK" are imported as stocks, and those
                    with "FUND" as funds. IBKR reports mutual funds and some ETFs as "FUND", but many ETFs are reported as
                    "STK", so they appear as stocks.
                </li>
                <li><strong>FreeTrade, Trading 212 and Interactive Investor:</strong> every trade is imported as a stock.</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Manual entries:</strong> trades missing from your files can be added on the
                    <a href="AddTradePage" class="link-styled">Add Manual Entries</a> page.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                On the <a href="MainCalculatorPage" class="link-styled">Import data</a> page you can choose which kinds of
                trade and income to load, for example to leave out funds or foreign currency.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">How they are calculated</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Stocks and funds use all the <a href="HowItWorks/same-day-rule" class="link-styled">matching rules</a>, with
                    one <a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a> per
                    <a href="HowItWorks/share-identities" class="link-styled">share identity</a>. Fees are handled as in
                    <a href="HowItWorks/net-proceeds" class="link-styled">Net proceeds and fees</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Both are reported under <strong>"Listed shares and securities"</strong> in the CGT Yearly Summary. Options,
                    futures and foreign currency go under "Other property, assets and gains".
                </li>
                <li>
                    Whether a holding is a fund matters for income. On the
                    <a href="ConvertPage" class="link-styled">Income taxable dividend selection</a> page you can treat a fund's
                    dividends as interest, and offshore reporting funds can have
                    <a href="HowItWorks/reporting-funds" class="link-styled">excess reportable income</a>.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/dividends" class="link-styled">Dividends</a>: income from shares and funds</li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/limitations" class="link-styled">What the calculator does not handle</a>: including non-reporting offshore funds</li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
